    "envy": {
      "word": "ENVY",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Malice; ill will; spite. [Obs.] If he evade us there, Enforce him with his envy to the people. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Chagrin, mortification, discontent, or uneasiness at the sight of another's excellence or good fortune, accompanied with some degree of hatred and a desire to possess equal advantages; malicious grudging; Envy is a repining at the prosperity or good of another, or anger and displeasure at any good of another which we want, or any advantage another hath above us. Ray. No bliss Enjoyed by us excites his envy more. Milton. Envy, to which the ignoble mind's a slave, Is emulation in the learned or brave. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Emulation; rivalry. [Obs.] Such as cleanliness and decency Prompt to a virtuous envy. Ford.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Public odium; ill repute. [Obs.] To lay the envy of the war upon Cicero. B. Jonson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
